    {
      "hint_id": 2,
      "train_position": 28,
      "train_qid": "30302",
      "hint": "For equations like (z-a)^n = c(z-b)^n, do not enumerate the n roots. Take moduli first: every root obeys the single condition |z-a| = k|z-b|, where k = |c|^(1/n). If k = 1 the locus is the perpendicular bisector of a and b (a line, not a circle); otherwise it is an Apollonius circle. Get its data algebraically: put z = x+iy, square both sides, expand, collect the (1-k^2)(x^2+y^2) term, divide by that coefficient, and complete the square to read off center and radius. Expect a center away from the origin, so |z| varies along the locus; varying |z| does NOT mean the locus fails to be a circle. Confirm dividing by (z-b)^n dropped no root, then report exactly the quantity asked (radius, center, or diameter).",
